  "title": "Tools, data, and formulas",
  "body": " Tools, data, and formulas   Tools   In the tool samples are drawn without replacement. To carry out bootstrapping (sampling with replacement), a bootstrap distribution made of many copies of a set of sample data should be loaded into the tool as a .csv file and should then be used as the population distribution.    The tool bins data using the Freedman-Diaconis rule, which may produce different looking histograms when loading data from the sections.    Table 3101: One mean relevant features by section    Section  Feature    Sampling from a finite population  Draw samples from a finite population    Inference for a single quantitative variable  Input parameters    Theory-based Inference for a Population Mean   Calculate simulation-based and theory-based p-value and standardized statistic for means  with specified alternative hypothesis     Other statistics  Simulation-based analysis for medians    Tools, data, and formulas   Specify statistic as \"means\" or \"medians\"  Input hypothetical population by specifying shape, mean, and standard deviation  Input hypothetical population by uploading a .csv file        Data   References    Section  Data  Reference    Example: Sampling from a finite population  =0.592, n=2181, =0.698  US Elections Project (2016) General Social Survey (2018)    Supplemental Exploration: Sampling from a finite population  =0.41  US Library of Congress    Example: Inference for a single quantitative variable  CollegeMidwestPopulation.csv     Supplemental Exploration: Inference for a single quantitative variable  Gettysburg.csv  US Library of Congress    Example: Theory-based Inference for a Population Mean  SnippetEstimate.csv n=48, =13.71, =6.5  SnippetEstimatePopulation.csv     Supplemental Exploration: Theory-based Inference for a Population Mean  SleepTimes.csv n=22, =6.705, =1.267  SleepTimesPopulation.csv     Example: Other statistics  SnippetEstimatePopulation.csv Population: skewed right, mean=10, sd=6.5     Supplemental Exploration: Other statistics  BackpackWeights.csv n=100, =7.712, =3.646  BackpackWeightsPopulation.csv  STATS (2002)    Investigation: Faking cell phone calls  =0.10, n=1858, =0.13  Pew (2011) IBTimes (2011)       Formulas for one mean    is the population mean.  is the population standard deviation.  is the sample size.  is the sample mean.  is the sample standard deviation.  is the theory-based standard deviation of the null distribution.  is the theory-based standard error, which approximates .   is the number of standard deviations away from the mean of the sampling distribution a statistic is.  is the simulation-based standardized statistic for one mean.  is the theory-based standardized statistic for one mean.   is the simulation-based standardized statistic for one mean.  is the theory-based standardized statistic for one mean.    "
