  "body": " Data and Formulas   Learning Goals    Compare two sample means numerically and graphically.  Carry out a simulation-based analysis to investigate the difference between two population means.  Carry out a theory-based analysis to investigate the difference between two population means.      Data   References       Section  Data  Reference    Example: Comparing two groups: Quantitative response  OldFaithful.csv  GOSA (1978) GOSA (2003)    Supplemental Exploration: Comparing two groups: Quantitative response   Short et al. (JSE 2017)    Example: Comparing two means: Simulation-based approach  DungBeetles.csv  Dacke et al. (Curr. Biol. 2013)    Supplemental Exploration: Comparing two means: Simulation-based approach  Sleep.csv  Stickgold et al.(Nat. Neurosci. 2000)    Example: Comparing two means: Theory-based approach  VideogameAgression.csv  Hollingdale and Greitemeyer (PLOS ONE 2014)    Supplemental Exploration: Comparing two means: Theory-based approach  CloseFriends.csv  General Social Survey (2004)    Investigation: Memorizing letters  memorizing_letters.csv        Formulas for two means    and are the population means for the two groups.  and are the sample sizes for the two groups.  and are the sample means for the two groups.  and are the sample standard deviations for the two groups.  is the standard deviation of the statistic.  is the theory-based standard error that approximates SD of the null distribution and .    is the number of standard deviations away from the mean of the sampling distribution a statistic is.  is the simulation-based standardized statistic for two independent means.  is the theory-based standardized statistic for two independent means.   is the simulation-based standardized statistic for two independent means.  is the theory-based standardized statistic for two independent means.   is a confidence interval.  is a 2SD CI approximating a 95% confidence interval estimating .  is a theory-based confidence interval estimating , where the multiplier comes from a t distribution with degrees of freedom.   is a 2SD CI approximating a 95% confidence interval estimating .  is a theory-based confidence interval estimating , where the multiplier comes from a t distribution with degrees of freedom.    "
