  "body": "   Pairing can also be used in the design of an observational study. Revisit the observational study in #2, and suppose also that you have times in the hundred yard dash for each of the 20 players. Explain how you could use those times to create pairs. (Suppose that, of the 20 players, 12 used the wide angle and only 8 used the narrow angle. This means you can create only 8 pairs.)    Use the time of the 100-yard dash from the 8 players who rounded first with the narrow angle and match those times to the closest 8 times out of the 12 players who ran the wide angle. That will give 8 matched pairs. We didn't assign the angle, but we can still try to compare the most similar runners to each other.   "
