  "body": " Data and Formulas   Learning Goals   Distinguish between the following study designs for comparing two groups on a quantitative response: observational, randomly assigned independent groups, matched pairs, repeated measures.  Perform and interpret a simulation-based analysis of paired data for a quantitative response.  Perform and interpret a theory-based analysis of paired data for a quantitative response.     Data   References    Section  Data  Reference    Example: Paired designs   Strauss (2009)    Supplemental Exploration: Paired designs  FirstBase.csv  Woodward (1970)    Example: Simulation-based approach for analyzing paired data  FirstBase.csv  Woodward (1970)    Supplemental Exploration: Simulation-based approach for analyzing paired data  JJvsBicycle.csv     Example: Theory-based approach for analyzing paired data  DadJokes.csv  Cai et al. (Curr. Biol. 2019)    Supplemental Exploration: Theory-based approach for analyzing paired data  Auction.csv  Lucking-Reiley (Am. Econ. Rev. 1999)    Investigation: Filtering water in Cameroon  Ecoli_time.csv  Ecoli_sand.csv   Ecoli_flow.csv   Hope College (2007)       Formulas for two means  Independent samples:   and are the population means for the two groups.  and are the sample sizes for the two groups.  and are the sample means for the two groups.  and are the sample standard deviations for the two groups.  is the standard deviation of the statistic.  is the theory-based standard error that approximates SD of the null distribution and .    is the number of standard deviations away from the mean of the sampling distribution a statistic is.  is the simulation-based standardized statistic for two independent means.  is the theory-based standardized statistic for two independent means.   is the simulation-based standardized statistic for two independent means.  is the theory-based standardized statistic for two independent means.   is a confidence interval.  is a 2SD CI approximating a 95% confidence interval estimating .  is a theory-based confidence interval estimating , where the multiplier comes from a t distribution with degrees of freedom.   is a 2SD CI approximating a 95% confidence interval estimating .  is a theory-based confidence interval estimating , where the multiplier comes from a t distribution with degrees of freedom.   Matched pairs:   is the variable of differences for each matched pair.  is the population mean difference.  is the sample size (number of matched pairs).  is the sample mean of the differences.  is the sample standard deviation of the differences.  is the standard deviation of the statistic.  is the theory-based standard error that approximates SD of the null distribution and .    is the number of standard deviations away from the mean of the sampling distribution a statistic is.  is the simulation-based standardized statistic for matched pairs.  is the theory-based standardized statistic for matched pairs.   is the simulation-based standardized statistic for matched pairs.  is the theory-based standardized statistic for matched pairs.   is a confidence interval.  is a 2SD CI approximating a 95% confidence interval estimating .  is a theory-based confidence interval estimating , where the multiplier comes from a t distribution with degrees of freedom.   is a 2SD CI approximating a 95% confidence interval estimating .  is a theory-based confidence interval estimating , where the multiplier comes from a t distribution with degrees of freedom.    "
