  "title": "",
  "body": "   Open the Corr\/Regression applet . Copy the data from DraftLottery into the applet (remember to include the column titles). Check the Correlation Coefficient box and confirm that the correlation coefficient is -0.226.     Check the Show Shuffle Options box and select Correlation from the Statistic pull-down menu to the right to keep track of that statistic. Then press Shuffle Y-values to simulate one fair, random lottery. Record the value of the correlation coefficient between the shuffled draft numbers and sequential date.    After one shuffle, answers will vary.      Press Shuffle Y-values four more times to generate results of four more fair, random lotteries. Record the values of the shuffled correlation coefficients in a table.    Answers will vary, for example: 0.002, 0.039, -0.027, 0.09, -0.039      Did any of your simulated statistics from fair, random lotteries produce a correlation coefficient as extreme (far from zero) as the observed -0.226?    No.      Change the Number of Shuffles from 1 to 995 and press Shuffle Y-values to simulate 995 more fair, random lotteries. Look at the null distribution of these 1,000 correlation coefficients. Where is this distribution centered? Why does this make sense?    Center is 0. If the null hypothesis is true, that means there is no underlying association between the two variables, so we would expect correlations around zero.      Next to the Count Samples box choose Beyond from the pull-down menu. Specify the observed correlation coefficient (-0.226) and press Count. What proportion, of the 1,000 simulated random lotteries produced a correlation coefficient as extreme (as far from zero in either direction) as -0.226? Report the approximate p-value.    The approximate p-value is 0.      Interpret this p-value: This is the probability of what, assuming what?    The probability of getting a correlation coefficient of -0.226 or more extreme (in either direction) assuming the null hypothesis of no association is true.      What conclusion would you draw from this p-value? Do you have strong evidence that the 1970 draft lottery was not conducted with a fair, random process? Explain the reasoning behind your conclusion.    The p-value of zero offers very strong evidence against the null hypothesis and in support of the alternative hypothesis that states there is an association between draft lottery number and sequential birth date. In all the shufflings and recalculations of simulated correlation coefficients, not one was as extreme as -0.226 from the study.    "
